WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.475
Lesson 14.6.

00:00:02.475 --> 00:00:06.040
Searchers, keepers and arbitrage bots: how they work.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.535
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:08.535 --> 00:00:12.099
to understand the professional bot economy that shapes DeFi

00:00:12.099 --> 00:00:14.475
prices, and why competing in it is hard.

00:00:15.775 --> 00:00:17.065
Here's the plan.

00:00:17.065 --> 00:00:18.750
First, the key ideas.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.465
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:21.465 --> 00:00:22.990
Then, your checklist.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:25.152
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:26.452 --> 00:00:29.769
First, Searchers run bots that find and capture

00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:35.091
opportunities: DEX arbitrage (price gaps between pools/venues), liquidations

00:00:35.091 --> 00:00:38.824
(repay debt, take the bonus), backruns (trading right

00:00:38.824 --> 00:00:42.441
after a large swap), and, harmfully, sandwiches.

00:00:42.441 --> 00:00:46.141
Next, Keepers perform maintenance jobs for protocols

00:00:46.141 --> 00:00:50.071
(liquidations, auction bids, rebalancing) for a fee.

00:00:50.071 --> 00:00:52.776
Then, the economics: bots bid for

00:00:52.776 --> 00:00:56.736
inclusion by paying builders/validators (2.8).

00:00:56.736 --> 00:01:00.795
Competition drives profits towards zero; the winners have the

00:01:00.795 --> 00:01:05.546
lowest latency, best infrastructure, private order flow and capital.

00:01:05.546 --> 00:01:08.936
Also, why it matters to you: these bots are

00:01:08.936 --> 00:01:12.711
why pool prices track the market (and cause LVR,

00:01:12.711 --> 00:01:17.334
2.7), why liquidations happen within seconds (3.3), and why

00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:21.486
tight slippage and protected routing matter (2.5).

00:01:21.486 --> 00:01:24.650
And finally, for a learner: build read-only

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:28.186
monitors (14.5) and understand the mechanics.

00:01:28.186 --> 00:01:32.135
Running competitive bots is a professional engineering business

00:01:32.135 --> 00:01:35.281
with real losses from failed transactions and bugs.

00:01:36.581 --> 00:01:39.096
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:39.096 --> 00:01:44.068
A liquidation worth a $300 bonus (3.3) appears.

00:01:44.068 --> 00:01:46.182
Dozens of bots see it in the same

00:01:46.182 --> 00:01:48.668
block; the winner pays most of the $300

00:01:48.668 --> 00:01:51.579
to the block builder to be included first.

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:53.984
The "profit" left after fees and gas

00:01:53.984 --> 00:01:56.259
might be a few dollars, and losing

00:01:56.259 --> 00:01:58.054
bots may still pay gas.

00:01:58.054 --> 00:02:01.217
That's why liquidations are near-instant, and why your

00:02:01.217 --> 00:02:04.264
buffer (not a liquidator's delay) is your protection.

00:02:05.564 --> 00:02:07.024
Here's your checklist.

00:02:07.024 --> 00:02:08.559
Do it now, for real.

00:02:08.559 --> 00:02:10.542
First, I can name the main searcher

00:02:10.542 --> 00:02:12.714
strategies and their effect on me.

00:02:12.714 --> 00:02:16.668
And finally, My protections (slippage, private routing,

00:02:16.668 --> 00:02:19.704
HF buffers) assume bots act within seconds.

00:02:21.004 --> 00:02:22.214
Question 1.

00:02:22.214 --> 00:02:24.984
Where does most of a competitive searcher's profit go?

00:02:29.404 --> 00:02:33.444
The answer: To builders/validators, through bids for inclusion.

00:02:34.744 --> 00:02:35.989
Question 2.

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:37.234
What do keepers do?

00:02:41.654 --> 00:02:46.894
The answer: Paid maintenance for protocols: liquidations, auctions, rebalancing.

00:02:48.194 --> 00:02:49.464
Question 3.

00:02:49.464 --> 00:02:51.719
Why do liquidations happen so fast?

00:02:56.139 --> 00:02:59.424
The answer: Competing bots race to capture the bonus.

00:03:00.724 --> 00:03:01.969
Let's recap.

00:03:01.969 --> 00:03:05.634
Searchers run bots that find and capture opportunities.

00:03:05.634 --> 00:03:09.868
Keepers perform maintenance jobs for protocols (liquidations,

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:12.694
auction bids, rebalancing) for a fee.

00:03:12.694 --> 00:03:14.879
The economics: bots bid for

00:03:14.879 --> 00:03:18.769
inclusion by paying builders/validators (2.8).

00:03:18.769 --> 00:03:21.967
Why it matters to you: these bots are why

00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:25.774
pool prices track the market (and cause LVR, 2.7).

00:03:27.074 --> 00:03:29.289
Do the checklist now, before you move on.
